lare; the report-less cancellation and clean-close spot-check arms declare none); `compose` fills it through `prompts.fill`'s strict both-ways check and `agent_loop.session_body` is the single fork BOTH routing arms take, so a claimed adjudication row gets the same brief whether or not a routing registry is configured. Each assembler returns `(values, None)` or `(None, reason)` and NEVER a partial: `disposition_values` walks SpecRef -> the closed spec -> its immutable per-close report -> that report's TYPED `commit_range` -> `git log`/`--name-status` clipped at the declared 80 lines, and refuses the clean-close spot-check arm because it writes no report; `red_tc_values` RE-RUNS `census.red_tc_census` rather than remembering it, joins each line through `census.parse_red_tc` to the TC row's Method/Expected/Evidence and to the SR/LLR text its targets name, and refuses a census that has come clean. A refusal is a HOLD, never a fallback: `session_body` returns the reason and `agent_loop.route_session` prints it on the stop banner and returns `EXIT_NEEDS_HUMAN` with no session run, so a judge is never briefed as a builder; only a row declaring no brief builds from the worker assignment. EVERY shipped brief is routed through an assembler, including done-when and combined: `conflict` was RETIRED rather than filled (it had a template and a verdict grammar and never a mint, an assembler or a reader for the `needs=` its own grammar demanded), and `consolidate_values` composes the consolidation brief from the row's `Adjudicates` cluster re-derived LIVE - every row of that cluster or none, and a cluster whose overlap has dissolved refuses rather than briefing a session about a contradiction that no longer exists. `amendment_values` renders the approved-cell before/after of the drifted rows `trace.reattest_model` finds, BOUNDED by the row's `Adjudicates` scope, which the amendment mint writes from the rows it routes exactly as the first-approval mint does: a row declaring no scope refuses rather than reading the empty cell as the whole tree, a scope none of whose rows still differs from its approved copy refuses naming them, and a row hanging under several SRs is rendered once, naming each chain; its baseline line names, for each registry shown, the commit that last wrote that registry's snapshot copy (`baseline_snapshot.stamp` given the registry), never the newest write anywhere in the snapshot directory. For need, assumption and surrogate scopes, _unchained_amended_rows renders their drifted approved cells through tier_owing, and the amendment aftermath takes its held-rung arm from adjudication_action. QUESTION HOME. `tier_questions` reads `prompts/spine-questions.md` at render time for the `{questions}` slot that `first_approval_values` and `amendment_values` return. Each `## ` section's next line declares its served tiers as `<!-- tiers: ... -->`, with `*` serving every tier. It composes, in home order and without that declaration line, every-tier sections and sections serving the rows judged: `_judged_tiers` derives first-approval tiers from its `--approves` registry walk, and amendment tiers from the rows shown. `combined_values` composes each section through `compose`, so its first-approval and amendment sections carry their own questions. An absent or unreadable home, a section without a tiers declaration, a home with no section, or a home serving none of the judged tiers refuses the brief naming that home; there is no fallback.\"","+detail = \"The route reads `Brief` and `Adjudicates` through the same normalization the merge uses, so a spelling or scope accepted by one cannot change the other’s bound. The row's DECLARED `Brief` cell selects the template (`intake` writes it at every adjudication mint that has a brief to declare; the report-less cancellation and clean-close spot-check arms declare none); `compose` fills it through `prompts.fill`'s strict both-ways check and `agent_loop.session_body` is the single fork BOTH routing arms take, so a claimed adjudication row gets the same brief whether or not a routing registry is configured. Each assembler returns `(values, None)` or `(None, reason)` and NEVER a partial: `disposition_values` walks SpecRef -> the closed spec -> its immutable per-close report -> that report's TYPED `commit_range` -> `git log`/`--name-status` clipped at the declared 80 lines, and refuses the clean-close spot-check arm because it writes no report; `red_tc_values` RE-RUNS `census.red_tc_census` rather than remembering it, joins each line through `census.parse_red_tc` to the TC row's Method/Expected/Evidence and to the SR/LLR text its targets name, and refuses a census that has come clean. A refusal is a HOLD, never a fallback: `session_body` returns the reason and `agent_loop.route_session` prints it on the stop banner and returns `EXIT_NEEDS_HUMAN` with no session run, so a judge is never briefed as a builder; only a row declaring no brief builds from the worker assignment. EVERY shipped brief is routed through an assembler, including done-when and combined: `conflict` was RETIRED rather than filled (it had a template and a verdict grammar and never a mint, an assembler or a reader for the `needs=` its own grammar demanded), and `consolidate_values` composes the consolidation brief from the row's `Adjudicates` cluster re-derived LIVE - every row of that cluster or none, and a cluster whose overlap has dissolved refuses rather than briefing a session about a contradiction that no longer exists. `amendment_values` renders the approved-cell before/after of the drifted rows `trace.reattest_model` finds, BOUNDED by the row's `Adjudicates` scope, which the amendment mint writes from the rows it routes exactly as the first-approval mint does: a row declaring no scope refuses rather than reading the empty cell as the whole tree, a scope none of whose rows still differs from its approved copy refuses naming them, and a row hanging under several SRs is rendered once, naming each chain; its baseline line names, for each registry shown, the commit that last wrote that registry's snapshot copy (`baseline_snapshot.stamp` given the registry), never the newest write anywhere in the snapshot directory. For need, assumption and surrogate scopes, _unchained_amended_rows renders their drifted approved cells through tier_owing, and the amendment aftermath takes its held-rung arm from adjudication_action. QUESTION HOME. `tier_questions` reads `prompts/spine-questions.md` at render time for the `{questions}` slot that `first_approval_values` and `amendment_values` return. Each `## ` section's next line declares its served tiers as `<!-- tiers: ... -->`, with `*` serving every tier. It composes, in home order and without that declaration line, every-tier sections and sections serving the rows judged: `_judged_tiers` derives first-approval tiers from its `--approves` registry walk, and amendment tiers from the rows shown. `combined_values` composes each section through `compose`, so its first-approval and amendment sections carry their own questions. An absent or unreadable home, a section without a tiers declaration, a home with no section, or a home serving none of the judged tiers refuses the brief naming that home; there is no fallback. Judging briefs order no act: a first-approval brief derives neither an approval-registry token nor its per-token row map, and amendment aftermath identifies each verdict mark for a later turn. A CLARITY verdict is later re-anchored, naming its verdict on a held rung; a released-rung MEANING verdict it blesses is marked `MEANING+BLESS`; a held MEANING verdict is recommended to the owner.\""," rationale = \"\"\"Two rules decide the shape. A judge's brief must not carry the claim under judgement as its premise - a leaked self-assessment anchors the judgement to it - so every value is registry-, git- or report-derived and the one claim shown - the lane's report - is the template's own labelled subject. And a HALF-filled brief is worse than the generic prompt: hollow sections read as an investigation that was run and found nothing, which is the expensive way to be wrong, so there is no placeholder path at all. The discriminator is a declared cell rather than a SpecRef shape because that inference is unsound - an amendment to a test-case row and a red-TC census row both cite the TC registry while their briefs give contradictory instructions - and rather than a Title substring because that is the `NEEDS-HUMAN` fold (LLR-161).\"\"\""," test_refs = \"TC-161; TC-331\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":3234,"oldLines":8,"newStart":3234,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-227\", \"SR-239\"]"," title = \"Coordinator adjudication shares the keep request and refuses an unsigned retained home\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/session_service.py;project-trajectory/scripts/session_keep.py;project-trajectory/scripts/adjudicate_brief.py\"","-code_symbol = \"AdjudicationRequest/adjudication_keep/SigninRefused/SIGNIN_PROBES/TOKEN_VARIABLES/COMPETING_CREDENTIALS/RUNNER_REFUSAL/_read_token/_token_refusal/launch_credential/refuse_competing/_CASE_INSENSITIVE/_name_key/Prepared/prepare_launch/_resolve_executable/probe_env/compose_env/_compose/_runner_argv/_as_prepared/_launch_env/_redactor/signin_status/require_signin/route_env;dedicated_home;governing_templates\"","-detail = \"REQUEST. AdjudicationRequest carries root, brief, the selected route row, work item, role, prompt-template overrides and deadline. Both callers compose that request once; adjudication_keep derives governing templates, then plans with the selected row. PREPARATION. prepare_launch reads no registry: from that row it derives family, template, model, tier, declared environment, dedicated home and launch credential. A token family is signed-in exactly when the file its declared environment variable names is present, readable and nonempty; its probe runs no command. dedicated_home resolves every dedicated home without creating it. For an ordinary dedicated home, only documented command-run pairs count: Codex accepts exactly one Logged in using <method> line at exit 0 or exactly Not logged in at exit 1, apart from blank lines and its PATH-alias warning; Claude without a token accepts exactly one JSON object with boolean loggedIn true at exit 0 or false at exit 1; another answer, failure or timeout is unknown. An absent, unreadable or empty token file refuses before a lease, naming the declared variable and the setup step but never its path or value. refuse_competing refuses a token route declaring any competing credential name, compared by _name_key under platform case rules, or carrying --bare; settings-file credential sources are outside this boundary. compose_env builds the launch environment by removing names the launch sets and competing credential names, then applying declared pairs, home and the token as the launch's one environment credential. Prepared carries the template's first token after normal substitution, resolved once to an absolute executable on that environment's PATH; failure refuses before a lease and names the runner and declared or ambient PATH. cli_version and the retained launch run that executable; _as_prepared makes act launch and account the selected row rather than caller copies. probe_env composes the prepared environment with the launch credential withheld. OUTPUT. act redacts each verbatim occurrence of the credential value from physical output lines and captured stream before result, usage, logs or records consume it; escaped, encoded and line-split representations are outside that bound. No registry reread, fallback or automatic sign-in occurs. AdjudicationRequest also carries scope. governing_templates receives the request role: for REVIEW it returns the once-resolved reviewer template text used by the brief, and for ADJUDICATE it keeps the retained-class identity; adjudication_keep passes that role.\"","+code_symbol = \"AdjudicationRequest/adju